.SH Transitions
A transition determines how the machine moves between states.
.SS Basic Transition Syntax
.IP
.EX
transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.counter == 5\f[I]    # Condition (target language expression)\f[R]
    action\f[B]: \f[R]|\f[I]                  # Code executed during the transition\f[R]
      // runs BEFORE exit/entry sequence
    to\f[B]:\f[R] target_state\f[I]           # Where to go\f[R]
.EE
.PP
All three fields are optional:
.IP \(bu 2
Omitting \f[CR]guard\f[R] makes the transition unconditional
(\f[CR]true\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
Omitting \f[CR]action\f[R] means no transition\-time code.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]to: null\f[R] triggers machine termination.
.SS Transition Execution Order
When a transition fires:
.IP "1." 3
Evaluate the \f[CR]guard\f[R] condition.
.IP "2." 3
Set \f[CR]transition_fired = true\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
Execute \f[CR]action\f[R] code (if any).
.IP "4." 3
Execute the exit sequence (from current state up to the Least Common
Ancestor, bottom\-up).
.IP "5." 3
Execute the entry sequence (from LCA down to target state, top\-down).
.SS Using Actions (The Mealy Aspect)
The \f[CR]action\f[R] keyword allows you to execute code
\f[I]during\f[R] the transition, before the old state is exited.
.IP
.EX
states\f[B]:\f[R]
  reading\f[B]:\f[R]
    transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.value > 100
        action\f[B]: \f[R]|
          println!(\(dqMoving {} \-> {}\(dq, t_src, t_dst);
          ctx.log_alert(\(dqThreshold Exceeded\(dq);
        to\f[B]:\f[R] alert_mode
.EE
.IP
.EX
states\f[B]:\f[R]
  reading\f[B]:\f[R]
    transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx\->value > 100
        action\f[B]: \f[R]|
          printf(\(dqMoving %s \-> %s\(rsn\(dq, t_src, t_dst);
          log_alert(ctx, \(dqThreshold Exceeded\(dq);
        to\f[B]:\f[R] alert_mode
.EE
.SS Path Syntax
The \f[CR]to:\f[R] keyword supports several addressing modes:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
\f[B]Syntax\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[B]Description\f[R]
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]/absolute/path\f[R]
T}@T{
From the root.
Use for long jumps across the hierarchy.
T}
T{
\f[CR]sibling\f[R]
T}@T{
A state in the same parent (lateral transition).
T}
T{
\f[CR]./child\f[R]
T}@T{
A direct child of the current state (drill down).
T}
T{
\f[CR]../sibling\f[R]
T}@T{
Move up one level to the parent and find a sibling (a child of the
parent).
Equivalent to bare \f[CR]sibling\f[R].
Each additional \f[CR]../\f[R] climbs one more level, so
\f[CR]../../uncle\f[R] is a sibling of the parent, \f[CR]../../../x\f[R]
a sibling of the grandparent, and so on.
T}
T{
\f[CR].\f[R]
T}@T{
Self\-transition: the state exits and immediately re\-enters.
T}
T{
\f[CR]null\f[R]
T}@T{
Machine termination.
Exits all states up to root and stops.
T}
T{
\f[CR]\(atName\f[R]
T}@T{
Delegate to a named decision or AND pseudo\-state (see
Sections\ 10\(en11).
The path prefix follows exactly the same rules as for state paths: bare
\f[CR]\(atName\f[R] resolves in the calling state\(cqs parent container,
\f[CR]./Child/\(atName\f[R] goes into a child,
\f[CR]../Sibling/\(atName\f[R] steps up then down, \f[CR]/\(atName\f[R]
is an absolute root reference, etc.
T}
T{
\f[CR]/path/[a/tgt, b/tgt]\f[R]
T}@T{
Explicit fork into an orthogonal state (see Section\ 9).
T}
.TE
.SS Transition Scenarios
.SS Lateral (Sibling to Sibling)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Exits:\f[R] A
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Enters:\f[R] B
.SS Drill Down (Parent to Child)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Exits:\f[R] None (P remains active).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Enters:\f[R] C
.SS Move Up (Child to Parent/Uncle)
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Exits:\f[R] C, then Parent P.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]Enters:\f[R] U
.SS Termination
Setting \f[CR]to: null\f[R] causes the machine to exit all states from
the current state up through root, then stop.
After termination, \f[CR]is_running()\f[R] (Rust/Python),
\f[CR]isRunning()\f[R] (TypeScript), or \f[CR]sm_is_running()\f[R] (C)
returns false.
.SH History
A composite state with \f[CR]history: true\f[R] remembers its last
active child.
When re\-entered, it resumes from that child instead of the
\f[CR]initial\f[R] state.
.IP
.EX
process\f[B]:\f[R]
  history\f[B]:\f[R] true
  initial\f[B]:\f[R] step_a
  states\f[B]:\f[R]
    step_a\f[B]:\f[R]
      transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
        \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.counter == 3
          to\f[B]:\f[R] step_b
    step_b\f[B]:\f[R]
      transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
        \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.counter == 5
          to\f[B]:\f[R] /other_state
.EE
.PP
If the machine leaves \f[CR]process\f[R] while in \f[CR]step_b\f[R], and
later transitions back to \f[CR]process\f[R], it will re\-enter
\f[CR]step_b\f[R] directly (skipping \f[CR]step_a\f[R]).
.SH Orthogonal States (Parallelism)
An orthogonal state runs multiple child regions simultaneously.
.PD 0
.P
.PD
\f[B]Keyword:\f[R] \f[CR]orthogonal: true\f[R]
.IP
.EX
parallel_task\f[B]:\f[R]
  orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true
  states\f[B]:\f[R]
    region_a\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] idle_a
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        idle_a\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
        working_a\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
    region_b\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] idle_b
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        idle_b\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
        working_b\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
.EE
.PP
When \f[CR]parallel_task\f[R] is entered, \f[B]all\f[R] child regions
are entered simultaneously.
Each region ticks independently.
When exiting, all regions are exited.
.PP
The state path for orthogonal states uses bracket notation:
\f[CR]/parallel_task/[region_a/idle_a,region_b/idle_b]\f[R].
.SS Safety: \f[CR]transition_fired\f[R]
When a transition fires in one region, the other regions\(cq ticks are
skipped for that cycle to prevent inconsistent state.
This is handled automatically via the \f[CR]transition_fired\f[R] flag.
.SS Fork Transitions (Drilling Down)
You can enter an orthogonal state by targeting its children directly.
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Implicit Fork:\f[R] \f[CR]to: /run/g/a/sub_state\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
The generator forces region \f[CR]a\f[R] to \f[CR]sub_state\f[R].
Other regions start at their \f[CR]initial\f[R] states.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Explicit Fork:\f[R] \f[CR]to: /run/g/[a/sub_state, b/other]\f[R]
.PD 0
.P
.PD
Explicitly sets the target for multiple regions at once.
.SS Cross\-Limb Transitions
A transition from one region to another region within the same
orthogonal parent is a \f[B]cross\-limb transition\f[R].
The generator handles this by exiting the target region\(cqs current
state and entering the new target, while the source region remains
unaffected.
.SS Joining (Synchronization)
There are two ways to join parallel regions.
.PP
\f[B]Manual join (all versions):\f[R] Place a transition on the
\f[B]parent\f[R] or on any state that checks \f[CR]IN_STATE()\f[R] for
all regions simultaneously.
.IP
.EX
main_task\f[B]:\f[R]
  orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true
  states\f[B]:\f[R]
    A\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] working
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        working\f[B]:\f[R]
          transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
            \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.done_a
              to\f[B]:\f[R] finished
        finished\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
    B\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] working
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        working\f[B]:\f[R]
          transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
            \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.done_b
              to\f[B]:\f[R] finished
        finished\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]

\f[I]  # Transition on the parent container\f[R]
  transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] IN_STATE(root_main_task_A_finished) && IN_STATE(root_main_task_B_finished)
      to\f[B]:\f[R] next_step
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Use the \f[CR]IN_STATE(X)\f[R] macro in guards for
portable state checks.
In Rust and Python, it expands to \f[CR]ctx.in_state_X()\f[R].
In TypeScript, it expands to \f[CR]ctx.inState_X()\f[R].
In C, it expands to the \f[CR]IN_STATE_X\f[R] macro.
.PP
\f[B]AND join nodes
(\f[CB]SM\-builder\-version: \(dq0.6.0\(dq\f[B]+):\f[R] For a cleaner,
declaration\-based approach that composes across any number of sources
and any hierarchy depth, use an \f[CR]ands:\f[R] block (see
Section\ 11).
The compiler generates the compound \f[CR]IN_STATE\f[R] guards
automatically.
.SH Decisions (Logic Trees)
Decisions allow you to externalize branching logic.
A decision node can point to states, or \f[B]chain to other
decisions\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]Restriction:\f[R] You cannot use \f[CR]action\f[R] or
\f[CR]hooks\f[R] inside a decision block.
Actions must be placed on the transition \f[I]leading to\f[R] the
decision, or on the state transitions following it.
.SS Legacy Mode (before \f[CR]SM\-builder\-version: \(dq0.6.0\(dq\f[R])
Decisions are collected into a \f[B]flat global namespace\f[R]; names
must be unique across the entire model.
All \f[CR]\(at\f[R] references are bare (\f[CR]\(atName\f[R]) and look
up from this global dict.
.SS Hierarchical Mode (\f[CR]SM\-builder\-version: \(dq0.6.0\(dq\f[R] and later)
Add \f[CR]SM\-builder\-version: \(dq0.6.0\(dq\f[R] (or higher) at the
top of your file to enable hierarchical pseudo\-state scoping.
Each \f[CR]decisions:\f[R] block is owned by the state it is defined in,
and \f[CR]\(at\f[R] references are \f[B]path\-qualified\f[R] \(em they
resolve relative to the calling state\(cqs parent container, not a
global dict.
.PP
Benefits:
.IP \(bu 2
Multiple states can each define a \f[CR]\(atCheck\f[R] decision without
name collisions.
.IP \(bu 2
References are local by default but can cross container boundaries when
needed.
.PP
References to pseudo\-states follow the same path syntax as references
to states (see Section\ 7.4): the prefix (\f[CR]./\f[R], \f[CR]../\f[R],
\f[CR]/absolute/\f[R], bare sibling, etc.)
navigates to a container, and \f[CR]\(atName\f[R] selects the
pseudo\-state within it instead of a child state.
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Target paths inside decision \f[I]rules\f[R] (the
\f[CR]to:\f[R] fields inside the decision\(cqs own rule list) resolve
from the \f[B]decision\(cqs own scope\f[R], not from the calling state.
This is the same behaviour as in legacy mode.
.SS Defining Decisions
Decisions can be defined at the root level or within any state.
.IP
.EX
decisions\f[B]:\f[R]
  validate_input\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.input < 0
      to\f[B]:\f[R] /error/negative
    \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.input > 100
      to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq\(atcheck_permissions\(dq\f[I]   # Chain to another decision\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /process/calc\f[I]          # Default (no guard)\f[R]

  check_permissions\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.is_admin
      to\f[B]:\f[R] /admin/override
    \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /error/denied
.EE
.SS Using Decisions
Reference a decision with the \f[CR]\(at\f[R] prefix in the
\f[CR]to:\f[R] field:
.IP
.EX
transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.data_ready
    to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq\(atvalidate_input\(dq
.EE
.PP
Decisions can also be defined locally within a state and referenced with
a bare name from sibling states:
.IP
.EX
my_state\f[B]:\f[R]
  decisions\f[B]:\f[R]
    local_check\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.x > 0
        to\f[B]:\f[R] positive
      \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] negative
  initial\f[B]:\f[R] waiting
  states\f[B]:\f[R]
    waiting\f[B]:\f[R]
      transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
        \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq\(atlocal_check\(dq
    positive\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
    negative\f[B]:\f[R] \f[B]{}\f[R]
.EE
.SH AND Join Nodes
AND join nodes (available in
\f[CR]SM\-builder\-version: \(dq0.6.0\(dq\f[R] and later) provide a
declarative way to synchronize multiple concurrent activities.
An AND node fires only when \f[B]all\f[R] of its source states are
simultaneously active and all per\-source guards are satisfied.
This replaces the need to manually write \f[CR]IN_STATE()\f[R]
conjunctions or to chain decision nodes for synchronization.
.SS Defining AND Nodes
Use the \f[CR]ands:\f[R] block at any container level, with the same
structure as \f[CR]decisions:\f[R].
.IP
.EX
SM\-builder\-version\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq0.6.0\(dq
language\f[B]:\f[R] python

initial\f[B]:\f[R] Parallel

states\f[B]:\f[R]
  Parallel\f[B]:\f[R]
    orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true
    ands\f[B]:\f[R]
      J1\f[B]:\f[R]\f[I]              # Name of the AND join node\f[R]
        \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /Done\f[I]    # What to do when ALL sources are ready\f[R]
    states\f[B]:\f[R]
      RegA\f[B]:\f[R]
        initial\f[B]:\f[R] Working
        states\f[B]:\f[R]
          Working\f[B]:\f[R]
            transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
              \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 2
                to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq../../\(atJ1\(dq\f[I]   # Reference J1 two levels up (inside Parallel)\f[R]
      RegB\f[B]:\f[R]
        initial\f[B]:\f[R] Processing
        states\f[B]:\f[R]
          Processing\f[B]:\f[R]
            transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
              \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 3
                to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq../../\(atJ1\(dq
  Done\f[B]:\f[R]
    transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] null
.EE
.PP
The generated code for \f[CR]Working\f[R]\(cqs transition automatically
becomes:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Guard for Working \-> \(atJ1:\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] ctx.step >= 2:
    \f[B]if\f[R] ctx.in_state_root_Parallel_RegB_Processing() \f[B]and\f[R] ctx.step >= 3:
        \f[I]# \-\- fire AND\(aqs outgoing rule: to /Done \-\-\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The compound condition (\f[CR]in_state(Other) and other_guard\f[R]) is
generated automatically from the reverse\-edge information collected at
compile time.
No manual \f[CR]IN_STATE\f[R] conjunctions needed.
.SS Use Case 1 \(em Synchronization Join (Sibling Regions)
This is the most common pattern: two or more regions inside the same
orthogonal state each independently work toward a \(lqready\(rq
sub\-state, and the whole machine proceeds only when all regions are
ready simultaneously.
.IP
.EX
SM\-builder\-version\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq0.6.0\(dq

Parallel\f[B]:\f[R]
  orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true
  ands\f[B]:\f[R]
    AllDone\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /Final
  states\f[B]:\f[R]
    RegA\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] A
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        A\f[B]:\f[R]
          transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
            \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 2
              to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq../../\(atAllDone\(dq
    RegB\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] B
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        B\f[B]:\f[R]
          transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
            \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 3
              to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq../../\(atAllDone\(dq
    RegC\f[B]:\f[R]
      initial\f[B]:\f[R] C
      states\f[B]:\f[R]
        C\f[B]:\f[R]
          transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
            \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 4
              to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq../../\(atAllDone\(dq
.EE
.PP
The join fires at \f[CR]step = 4\f[R] \(em when the last region (RegC)
becomes ready.
At \f[CR]step = 2\f[R] and \f[CR]step = 3\f[R], the composite guard
blocks even though RegA or RegB are ready, because the other regions
have not yet satisfied their guards.
.PP
\f[B]Transition ordering is preserved\f[R]: if a source state has
higher\-priority transitions listed \f[I]before\f[R] its AND edge, those
transitions take precedence and the AND gate is suppressed for that
cycle, matching the normal UML priority rule.
.SS Use Case 2 \(em Cross\-Boundary Join (Any Combination of Sources)
AND sources do not need to be in the same orthogonal state.
They can come from any states anywhere in the tree \(em different
nesting depths, different branches, or even non\-orthogonal states.
.IP
.EX
SM\-builder\-version\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq0.6.0\(dq

ands\f[B]:\f[R]\f[I]            # Root\-level AND node\f[R]
  J1\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /Done

initial\f[B]:\f[R] Root
states\f[B]:\f[R]
  Root\f[B]:\f[R]
    orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true
    states\f[B]:\f[R]
      OrthoA\f[B]:\f[R]
        orthogonal\f[B]:\f[R] true\f[I]   # Nested orthogonal\f[R]
        states\f[B]:\f[R]
          Worker\f[B]:\f[R]
            initial\f[B]:\f[R] ActiveA
            states\f[B]:\f[R]
              ActiveA\f[B]:\f[R]
                transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
                  \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 2
                    to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq/\(atJ1\(dq\f[I]   # Absolute reference to root\-level AND\f[R]
      OrthoB\f[B]:\f[R]
        initial\f[B]:\f[R] ActiveB
        states\f[B]:\f[R]
          ActiveB\f[B]:\f[R]
            transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
              \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.step >= 3
                to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq/\(atJ1\(dq
  Done\f[B]:\f[R]
    transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] null
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]ActiveA\f[R] lives at depth 5 inside a doubly\-nested orthogonal
region; \f[CR]ActiveB\f[R] is at depth 4 in a sibling region.
Both reference the same root\-level join node via \f[CR]/\(atJ1\f[R].
The generated guard for \f[CR]ActiveA\f[R] is:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] ctx.step >= 2:
    \f[B]if\f[R] ctx.in_state_root_Root_OrthoB_ActiveB() \f[B]and\f[R] ctx.step >= 3:
        \f[I]# fire J1\(aqs rule: to /Done\f[R]
.EE
.SS Use Case 3 \(em Replacing Chained Decisions
Without AND nodes, synchronizing two flags often required chaining two
decisions:
.IP
.EX
decisions\f[B]:\f[R]
  check_both\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] \(dqIN_STATE(root_Parallel_RegA_Done) && IN_STATE(root_Parallel_RegB_Done)\(dq
      to\f[B]:\f[R] /Final
    \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] stay_here\f[I]  # dummy self\-loop\f[R]
.EE
.PP
With an AND node, the same semantics are expressed declaratively:
.IP
.EX
ands\f[B]:\f[R]
  BothDone\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] /Final

\f[I]# Then in each region\(aqs Done state:\f[R]
transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] to\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq\(atBothDone\(dq
.EE
.PP
The AND node is less error\-prone (no manual state name strings), adapts
automatically when states are renamed, and composes cleanly across N
sources without O(N) chaining.
.SS Per\-Source Guards and the \f[CR]time\f[R] Variable
Each incoming edge to an AND node may carry its own \f[CR]guard\f[R].
When the AND fires from source state X, the generated code ANDs in every
other source\(cqs \f[CR]in_state()\f[R] check \f[I]plus\f[R] that
source\(cqs guard expression.
.PP
If a guard uses the special \f[CR]time\f[R] variable, it is
automatically rebased to that source state\(cqs own entry timestamp, not
the firing state\(cqs timer:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# Guard in RegB: time >= 2.0  \->  fires if RegB has been active >= 2 s\f[R]
\f[I]# When RegA fires the join, the generated check for RegB is:\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] ctx.in_state_RegB() \f[B]and\f[R] (ctx.now \- ctx.state_timers[regb_id]) >= 2.0:
    ...
.EE
.SS AND Node Rules
AND outgoing rules follow exactly the same syntax as decision rules: a
list of \f[CR]{guard, to}\f[R] entries evaluated top\-to\-bottom.
The first matching rule fires.
Rules inside the AND resolve paths from the AND node\(cqs own scope (the
container it is defined in).
.SH Complete Example
Below is a minimal but complete SMB file demonstrating the key features,
with Rust as the target language.
.IP
.EX
language\f[B]:\f[R] rust

includes\f[B]: \f[R]|
  fn log(msg: &str) { println!(\(dq{}\(dq, msg); }

context\f[B]: \f[R]|
  pub counter: i32,

context_init\f[B]: \f[R]|
  counter: 0,

hooks\f[B]:\f[R]
  entry\f[B]: \f[R]|
    println!(\(dqEntering {}\(dq, state_full_name);
  exit\f[B]: \f[R]|
    println!(\(dqExiting {}\(dq, state_full_name);

initial\f[B]:\f[R] active

states\f[B]:\f[R]
  active\f[B]:\f[R]
    initial\f[B]:\f[R] idle
    states\f[B]:\f[R]
      idle\f[B]:\f[R]
        transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
          \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.counter >= 3
            to\f[B]:\f[R] working
      working\f[B]:\f[R]
        do\f[B]: \f[R]|
          ctx.counter += 1;
        transitions\f[B]:\f[R]
          \f[B]\-\f[R] guard\f[B]:\f[R] ctx.counter >= 10
            to\f[B]:\f[R] null\f[I]   # Terminate\f[R]

